# std::alloc_policy — allocator policy as a first-class library contract
# (audit item P1-12)
#
# The audit contract: hot paths need EXPLICIT guarantees about where the
# objects they touch come from — a slab allocator for the fixed-size
# object pools, a bump arena per market-data packet/batch, a
# preallocated order pool, a fixed-capacity ring, NO allocator in the
# matching loop (finance), and (embedded) a static arena + ArrayVec +
# fixed ring with no heap at all. Memory safety does not require a
# universal general-purpose allocator — it requires that every hot loop
# CHOOSES a policy and that the choice is a first-class, documented
# fact of the module that owns the loop.
#
# This module makes the policy a first-class library contract:
#   - AllocPolicy — the policy MARKERS (enum): Heap / Arena / Slab /
#     Fixed / None, with the audit's mapping spelled out on the enum;
#   - BumpArena — the deterministic bump (arena) allocator: owned
#     (new) or borrowed (with_buffer) backing, alloc/reset, no
#     individual free, zero-overhead reset;
#   - SlabPool[T] — the fixed-size typed object pool: preallocated
#     slots, acquire/release index hand-off over an intrusive free
#     list, no heap churn after init, exact drop of live occupants
#     with deterministic ascending teardown.
#
# WHERE THE POLICIES LIVE IN THE KERNEL STD (the call surface this
# module documents and marks):
#   - std::alloc (kernel family) is the low-level allocator authority:
#     trait Allocator, SystemAllocator (the runtime memory authority
#     behind _tg_mem_alloc/_tg_mem_free), ArenaAllocator/ArenaBuf
#     (policy: Arena), PoolAllocator (policy: Slab), the layout API and
#     the global alloc functions. Its alignment contract (any
#     power-of-two align <= 16; over-aligned requests are explicit
#     errors) is inherited verbatim by every allocation in this module.
#   - std::collections::Vec growth is HEAP by construction: push lowers
#     to the compiler intrinsic __intrinsic_array_push (the runtime
#     allocator), so a general allocator parameter on Vec[T, A] is
#     kernel type-system/codegen work — documented future work below.
#   - std::fixed (native family) delivers the policy Fixed/None forms:
#     ArrayVecN / RingBufferN / SpscQueueN with inline [Option[T]; N]
#     storage, no allocator, no locks.
#   - This module (std::alloc_policy) adds the policy markers and the
#     deterministic policy forms (BumpArena, SlabPool[T]) that the
#     audit's hot paths call directly.
#
# DROP DISCIPLINE (deallocate exactly once): an owner of MEMORY holds a
# raw pointer and declares the finalizer — the std::alloc Box/Arc and
# std::fixed SpscQueue split-endpoint record pattern. Both BumpArena
# and SlabPool are plain structs (raw Ptr + UInt fields) with a
# registered `impl Drop`: the Drop releases exactly the init-time
# allocations once and nulls every field, so a bit-copied owner cannot
# double-free (the std::alloc ArenaAllocator/PoolAllocator convention).
# A struct that owns VALUES holds them structurally and declares no
# finalizer — here the SlabPool occupant VALUES are owned by raw
# memory, so their destruction is the pool's own Drop/clear duty (each
# live occupant destructed exactly once, in ascending slot order).
#
# THREAD SAFETY: BumpArena and SlabPool are single-owner, NOT
# thread-safe. Nothing in this module locks; the per-pool free list and
# the bump pointer are plain fields of one owning value. (The runtime
# allocator's own locks behind global_alloc/global_dealloc apply only
# at init/teardown.)
#
# Module identity: std::alloc_policy (the file-based module — same
# convention as std/collections.tg and std/fixed.tg: no `module` block;
# the file name is the module path).

use std::core::{Option, Result, Unit, Bool, UInt, Int, panic}
use std::alloc::{global_alloc, global_dealloc, size_of, align_of}

# [METADATA]
# module: tg::alloc_policy
# status: alpha
# effects: []
# capabilities: []


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# AllocPolicy — the policy markers (the P1-12 audit contract in one type)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The audit contract, in one type: every hot path chooses ONE allocation
# policy up front, and the marker makes the choice a first-class,
# documented fact. The mapping the audit item names:
#
#   Heap  — general-purpose allocation. The std::alloc authority
#           (SystemAllocator / the global alloc / the runtime behind
#           String/Vec/Map growth) and today's Vec growth path (the
#           __intrinsic_array_push intrinsic). The default when no
#           fixed bound exists; never the choice INSIDE a bounded hot
#           loop.
#   Arena — the bump arena: BumpArena (this module) and std::alloc's
#           ArenaAllocator/ArenaBuf. Per market-data packet / per
#           batch: deterministic bump pointer, free-all at reset or
#           teardown, no individual free.
#   Slab  — the fixed-size object pool: SlabPool[T] (this module) and
#           std::alloc's PoolAllocator. Preallocated slots for
#           per-symbol / per-order state; acquire/release index
#           hand-off; no heap churn after init.
#   Fixed — the inline fixed-capacity containers of std::fixed
#           (ArrayVecN / RingBufferN / SpscQueueN): the capacity is
#           part of the type name, the storage is inline [Option[T]; N],
#           and there is no allocation after construction.
#   None  — no allocator in the path at all: fixed.tg containers,
#           BumpArena::with_buffer over a static buffer (embedded), and
#           the finance matching loop, which touches only preallocated
#           pools and rings and never calls an allocator.
#
# The audit's hot-path mapping (the sentence to quote in code review):
#   matching loop (finance)     -> None (or Fixed for bounded staging);
#                                  no allocator call exists in the loop
#   market-data packet / batch  -> Arena (BumpArena; free-all at the
#                                  packet/batch boundary)
#   per-symbol state / orders   -> Slab (SlabPool[T] / PoolAllocator:
#                                  preallocated, acquire/release only)
#   bounded queues              -> Fixed (the fixed-capacity rings)
#   embedded                    -> None + Fixed + with_buffer (static
#                                  arena, ArrayVec, fixed ring — no heap
#                                  at all after static init)
#   Vec growth                  -> Heap today (intrinsic growth); the
#                                  arena-provided builder and the
#                                  Vec[T, A] / Map[K, V, H, A] forms are
#                                  documented future work (module header)
enum AllocPolicy
  Heap
  Arena
  Slab
  Fixed
  None
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BumpArena — deterministic bump (arena) allocator
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The policy form of std::alloc's ArenaAllocator: identical alignment
# contract and identical bump math, plus the borrowed-buffer form
# (with_buffer) that makes the arena usable over a static/owned buffer
# with NO allocation of its own (embedded policy). BumpArena is
# NOT thread-safe. reset() is a single store — zero-overhead free-all;
# it never touches the buffer contents (no memset), so bump
# determinism across resets is exact: after a reset the same alloc
# sequence returns the same addresses.
#
# ALIGNMENT CONTRACT (inherited from std::alloc, the SAME policy as
# SystemAllocator/ArenaAllocator): the owned backing buffer comes from
# the global allocator, whose payload is 16-byte-aligned, so any
# power-of-two align <= 16 is satisfied by the aligned-offset math; an
# align 0, a non-power-of-two, or an align > 16 is an EXPLICIT error
# (panic). For with_buffer the CALLER supplies the backing and must
# provide a buffer aligned to the maximum align that will be requested
# (16 for the full contract); the arena cannot verify a borrowed
# buffer's alignment.
#
# Exhaustion: alloc returns a null pointer (never a partial bump); the
# caller observes it and resets or extends the arena — allocation
# failures inside a batch are a policy decision, not a panic. A
# zero-size alloc returns null without advancing the bump pointer
# (the std::alloc zero-size contract).

struct BumpArena
  _buffer: Ptr[u8]    # the backing buffer (owned by new, borrowed by with_buffer)
  _capacity: UInt     # total backing bytes
  _offset: UInt       # the bump pointer (next allocation offset)
  _owns: UInt         # 1 = new() owns the backing (Drop releases); 0 = borrowed
end

impl BumpArena
  # Owned arena: allocates the backing ONCE from the global allocator
  # (16-byte-aligned payload) and releases it exactly once at Drop.
  # Allocation happens only here — every later operation (alloc/reset)
  # is bump-pointer work with no allocator involvement.
  def new(capacity: UInt) -> BumpArena
    let buffer = global_alloc(capacity, 16)
    if capacity != 0 && buffer.is_null() then
      panic("BumpArena::new: allocation failed - out of memory")
    end
    BumpArena {
      _buffer: buffer,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _offset: 0,
      _owns: 1
    }
  end

  # Borrowed arena: bumps over a CALLER-OWNED buffer (static/global
  # memory for the embedded policy, a preallocated region, etc.). The
  # arena never frees it — Drop is a no-op on the backing — and the
  # caller must keep the buffer alive (and aligned) for the arena's
  # whole lifetime.
  def with_buffer(buffer: Ptr[u8], capacity: UInt) -> BumpArena
    BumpArena {
      _buffer: buffer,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _offset: 0,
      _owns: 0
    }
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity
  end

  # Bytes bumped so far (the deterministic bump pointer position).
  def used(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._offset
  end

  def remaining(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity - self._offset
  end

  # The raw backing buffer (owned or borrowed). Read-only access to the
  # arena's memory for the caller that needs to hand the batch out;
  # writes to it must respect the bump discipline (bytes above _offset
  # belong to the next alloc).
  def buffer(self: Self) -> Ptr[u8]
    self._buffer
  end

  # Allocate `size` bytes aligned to `align` (power of two <= 16).
  # Returns null on exhaustion; panics on an out-of-contract align.
  # No individual free exists — the whole arena is reclaimed by
  # reset() or by Drop.
  def alloc(inout self: Self, size: UInt, align: UInt) -> Ptr[u8]
    if align == 0 || (align & (align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("BumpArena::alloc: align must be a power of two")
    end
    if align > 16 then
      panic("BumpArena::alloc: align " + align.to_string() + " > 16 not supported - the backing buffer is 16-byte-aligned")
    end
    if size == 0 then
      return Ptr::null()
    end
    if self._buffer.is_null() || self._capacity == 0 then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    # Align the current bump offset (the same aligned-offset math as
    # std::alloc's ArenaAllocator, with the same overflow guards).
    let align_mask = align - 1
    let aligned_offset = (self._offset + align_mask) & ~align_mask

    if aligned_offset < self._offset then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    let end_offset = aligned_offset + size
    if end_offset < aligned_offset then
      return Ptr::null()
    end

    if end_offset > self._capacity then
      return Ptr::null()  # exhausted: the caller resets or extends
    end

    let ptr = self._buffer.offset(aligned_offset as Int)
    self._offset = end_offset
    ptr
  end

  # Zero-overhead free-all: ONE store to the bump pointer. The buffer
  # contents are untouched, so a reset-then-realloc cycle returns the
  # SAME addresses for the same sequence (deterministic bump).
  def reset(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    self._offset = 0
  end
end

impl Drop for BumpArena
  def drop(inout self: BumpArena) -> Unit
    # Release the backing exactly once (owned arenas only) so a
    # bit-copied arena cannot double-own (and double-free) the buffer;
    # idempotent with the field nulling below.
    if self._owns != 0 && !self._buffer.is_null() && self._capacity != 0 then
      global_dealloc(self._buffer, self._capacity, 16)
    end
    self._buffer = Ptr::null()
    self._capacity = 0
    self._offset = 0
    self._owns = 0
  end
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SlabPool[T] — fixed-size typed object pool (the slab policy)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Preallocated slots for the per-symbol / per-order hot path: init does
# the pool's allocations (the slot arena + the free-list/occupancy
# metadata); acquire/release/clear afterwards are pure free-list work —
# NO heap churn after init. SlabPool is NOT thread-safe.
#
# INDEX HAND-OFF: acquire(inout, sink item) consumes the item into a
# free slot and returns the slot INDEX; release(inout, index) moves the
# occupant back out (ownership transfers to the caller) and returns it.
# The index is an opaque handle into the pool — the language has no
# safe reference types, so value-in/value-out index hand-off is the
# sound surface (never a raw pointer into the slab). release on an
# out-of-range or already-vacant index returns None (a double release
# is a no-op, never a corruption).
#
# SLOT MEMORY + EXACT DROP: the slot arena is raw bytes; a slot holds a
# live T exactly while its occupancy flag is 1. acquire writes the item
# first and sets the flag LAST (the flag is the drop authority);
# release clears the flag FIRST after moving the value out. Teardown —
# both clear() and the registered Drop — walks the slots in ASCENDING
# index order and destructs every live occupant exactly once (clear
# through release-and-discard, Drop through drop_in_place on the live
# slots), then clear leaves a fully reusable empty pool and Drop
# releases the init-time allocations.
#
# The free list is intrusive over the metadata buffer: _free_next[i] is
# the next free slot after i, with the sentinel END = _capacity; the
# list is LIFO (release pushes onto the head), so a freshly released
# slot is the next one acquire hands out — deterministic reuse.
#
# ALIGNMENT CONTRACT (inherited from std::alloc): the slot arena comes
# from the global allocator (16-byte-aligned payload), so element types
# with align_of <= 16 are served; an element alignment above 16 is an
# EXPLICIT error at new() (the pool cannot honor over-aligned slots).

struct SlabPool[T]
  _slots: Ptr[u8]      # the element arena: capacity * stride bytes
  _stride: UInt        # element stride in bytes (size_of[T])
  _slots_bytes: UInt   # capacity * stride — the exact release size
  _free_next: Ptr[u8]  # free-list metadata: capacity UInt words (next[i])
  _live: Ptr[u8]       # occupancy metadata: capacity UInt words (0/1)
  _meta_bytes: UInt    # capacity * word — the exact release size
  _capacity: UInt      # slot count; also the free-list END sentinel
  _free_head: UInt     # free-list head; == _capacity when the pool is full
  _len: UInt           # live occupant count
end

impl[T] SlabPool[T]
  # Creates a pool of `capacity` preallocated slots. All pool
  # allocations happen here (slot arena + two metadata buffers, each
  # through the global allocator) and are released exactly once by
  # Drop. A zero capacity creates an empty pool that rejects every
  # acquire.
  def new(capacity: UInt) -> SlabPool[T]
    if capacity == 0 then
      return SlabPool {
        _slots: Ptr::null(),
        _stride: 0,
        _slots_bytes: 0,
        _free_next: Ptr::null(),
        _live: Ptr::null(),
        _meta_bytes: 0,
        _capacity: 0,
        _free_head: 0,
        _len: 0
      }
    end
    let elem_stride = size_of[T]()
    if elem_stride == 0 then
      panic("SlabPool::new: zero-size element types are not supported")
    end
    let elem_align = align_of[T]()
    if elem_align == 0 || (elem_align & (elem_align - 1)) != 0 then
      panic("SlabPool::new: element alignment must be a power of two")
    end
    if elem_align > 16 then
      panic("SlabPool::new: element alignment " + elem_align.to_string() + " > 16 not supported - the backing buffer is 16-byte-aligned")
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]()
    let slots_bytes = elem_stride * capacity
    if slots_bytes / capacity != elem_stride then
      panic("SlabPool::new: slot arena overflow")
    end
    let meta_bytes = word * capacity
    if meta_bytes / capacity != word then
      panic("SlabPool::new: metadata overflow")
    end
    let slots = global_alloc(slots_bytes, 16)
    let free_next = global_alloc(meta_bytes, 16)
    let live = global_alloc(meta_bytes, 16)
    if (slots_bytes != 0 && slots.is_null()) || free_next.is_null() || live.is_null() then
      panic("SlabPool::new: allocation failed - out of memory")
    end

    # Wire the free list (next[i] = i + 1, the last slot links to the
    # END sentinel) and clear every occupancy flag.
    mut i: UInt = 0
    while i < capacity do
      let next_ptr = (free_next as Int + (i as Int) * (word as Int)) as Ptr[UInt]
      next_ptr.write(i + 1)
      let live_ptr = (live as Int + (i as Int) * (word as Int)) as Ptr[UInt]
      live_ptr.write(0)
      i = i + 1
    end

    SlabPool {
      _slots: slots,
      _stride: elem_stride,
      _slots_bytes: slots_bytes,
      _free_next: free_next,
      _live: live,
      _meta_bytes: meta_bytes,
      _capacity: capacity,
      _free_head: 0,
      _len: 0
    }
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._capacity
  end

  # Live occupant count (occupied slots).
  def len(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._len
  end

  def is_empty(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len == 0
  end

  def is_full(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len >= self._capacity
  end

  # Whether the slot currently holds a live occupant.
  def occupied(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Bool
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return false
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    live_ptr.read() != 0
  end

  # Ownership-taking acquire: consumes the item into a free slot and
  # returns the slot index. A full pool (no free slot) does NOT consume
  # the item — it is returned to the caller as Err(item) (the
  # sink-rejected-value full policy of std::fixed). Allocation-free.
  def acquire(inout self: Self, sink item: T) -> Result[UInt, T]
    if self._free_head >= self._capacity then
      return Result::Err(item)
    end
    let idx = self._free_head
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let next_ptr = (self._free_next as Int + (idx as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    self._free_head = next_ptr.read()
    # The item lands in the slot BEFORE the occupancy flag is set (the
    # flag is the drop authority; nothing in between can panic).
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (idx as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    slot.write(item)
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (idx as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    live_ptr.write(1)
    self._len = self._len + 1
    Result::Ok(idx)
  end

  # Ownership-transferring release: moves the live occupant of `index`
  # out (the caller owns the returned value), clears the flag and
  # pushes the slot onto the free-list head (LIFO: the next acquire
  # reuses this slot — deterministic). None for an out-of-range or
  # already-vacant index (a double release is a no-op). Allocation-free.
  def release(inout self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return Option::None
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    if live_ptr.read() == 0 then
      return Option::None
    end
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (index as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    let value = slot.read()
    live_ptr.write(0)
    let next_ptr = (self._free_next as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    next_ptr.write(self._free_head)
    self._free_head = index
    self._len = self._len - 1
    Option::Some(value)
  end

  # Deterministic teardown of the live occupants: a single ascending
  # scan of the slots; every live occupant is released (its drop runs
  # as the returned Option is discarded — the std::fixed ArrayVec::clear
  # / std::collections VecDeque::clear drop convention) and every slot
  # returns to the free list. The pool is fully reusable afterwards
  # (len 0; acquires reuse the freed slots in LIFO order). Note: this
  # drops the occupant VALUES — an occupant that owns resources (a
  # String) releases its own memory through its own drop; the pool's
  # metadata itself is untouched by any allocator.
  def clear(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    mut i: UInt = 0
    while i < self._capacity do
      self.release(i)
      i = i + 1
    end
  end
end

# By-value projected read of an occupant (Copy only — sound
# duplication: the slot keeps its value; teardown still drops it).
impl[T: Copy] SlabPool[T]
  def get(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._capacity then
      return Option::None
    end
    let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
    let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (index as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
    if live_ptr.read() == 0 then
      return Option::None
    end
    let slot = (self._slots as Int + (index as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
    let value = slot.read()
    Option::Some(value)
  end
end

impl[T] Drop for SlabPool[T]
  def drop(inout self: SlabPool[T]) -> Unit
    # Teardown: destruct every live occupant EXACTLY ONCE — the same
    # deterministic ascending scan as clear(), over the occupancy
    # flags (the drop authority) — then release the three init-time
    # allocations and null every field, so a bit-copied pool cannot
    # double-free (the std::alloc ArenaAllocator/PoolAllocator
    # convention).
    if !self._slots.is_null() && self._capacity != 0 then
      let word = size_of[UInt]() as Int
      mut i: UInt = 0
      while i < self._capacity do
        let live_ptr = (self._live as Int + (i as Int) * word) as Ptr[UInt]
        if live_ptr.read() != 0 then
          let slot = (self._slots as Int + (i as Int) * (self._stride as Int)) as Ptr[T]
          slot.drop_in_place()
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
      global_dealloc(self._free_next, self._meta_bytes, 16)
      global_dealloc(self._live, self._meta_bytes, 16)
      global_dealloc(self._slots, self._slots_bytes, 16)
    end
    self._slots = Ptr::null()
    self._free_next = Ptr::null()
    self._live = Ptr::null()
    self._capacity = 0
    self._free_head = 0
    self._len = 0
    self._slots_bytes = 0
    self._meta_bytes = 0
  end
end

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Usage notes
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   use std::alloc_policy
#
#   # Per-batch bump arena (the market-data-packet policy): the packet
#   # decoder bumps its records out of one arena and free-alls at the
#   # packet boundary — no per-record allocator traffic:
#   var batch = std::alloc_policy::BumpArena::new(4096)
#   let hdr = batch.alloc(64, 8)
#   let body = batch.alloc(256, 16)
#   # ... fill the packet from hdr/body ...
#   batch.reset()   # one store reclaims the whole batch
#
#   # The per-symbol slab (orders/state records; preallocated once):
#   var pool = std::alloc_policy::SlabPool[Int].new(1024)
#   match pool.acquire(42)
#   when Result::Ok(slot) then
#     # ... use the opaque slot index as the record's handle ...
#     let _ = pool.release(slot)   # or release(slot) and own the value
#   when Result::Err(item) then ()  # pool full: the item returns
#   end
#
#   # The no-allocator matching loop (finance): a fixed-capacity ring
#   # funded from a preallocated slab — the loop body allocates NOTHING
#   # (policy: None/Fixed; the fill happens outside the match cycle):
#   var fund = std::alloc_policy::SlabPool[Int].new(8)
#   var ring = std::fixed::RingBuffer8[Int].new()
#   match fund.acquire(100)   # one prefill per ring slot
#   when Result::Ok(slot) then
#     let _ = ring.push(slot)
#   when Result::Err(_) then ()
#   end
#   # ... the matching loop pops ring / releases fund indices only ...
#
#   # Embedded/static (policy: None): the arena borrows a static buffer
#   # and the containers are std::fixed's inline forms — no heap at all.
#
# FUTURE WORK (documented gaps, kept OUT of this module's contract):
#   - A general allocator parameter on the growable containers
#     (Vec[T, A], Map[K, V, H, A]) needs kernel work: Vec growth lowers
#     to the __intrinsic_array_push intrinsic (the runtime allocator),
#     so an arena-provided Vec is not expressible from std today.
#     Bounded hot paths use this module's policy forms instead.
#   - Const-generic capacity (ArrayVecN-style forms spelled
#     ArrayVec[T, N]) is likewise kernel type-system work — see the
#     std::fixed header's compile-time-capacity decision.
